An RTOS provides built-in synchronization primitives like **mutexes** and **semaphores** to protect shared resources (e.g., global variables, hardware peripherals) from concurrent access.
Example: If Task A and Task B need to update a shared counter, a mutex can be used:
```c
osMutexAcquire(myMutex, osWaitForever);
shared_counter++;
osMutexRelease(myMutex);
```
This ensures only one task accesses `shared_counter` at a time, preventing race conditions, which would require manual interrupt disabling and careful timing in bare-metal.
